hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the most  central issues that Cicero grapples with  in his dialogue on friendship is the  basis of genuine friendship friendship  in the fullest sense and this was a  controversial matter back in ancient  times as it does remain so for many in  our own time so he's asking this  question and he recognizes it as very  important what is the source and basis  of true friendship and there's there's  more than one possible explanation for  this now one explanation that many  people give and this might actually be a  sign that you don't want to be friends  with them is that it's ultimately due to  needing something from the other person  and there's there's good ways to put  this and also you know bad ways to put  this or we might say less positive ways  when we talk about friendships  ultimately being merely transactional or  we suggest that not only two opposites  attract but it's its opposites within  the relationship that keep the  relationship going we're saying that one  person is supplying something that the  other person can't themselves provide  and  that's that's the reason why they're  attracted to each other and that's the  reason why they would stay with each  other and that's the reason why they  would do good things for each other and  that that's the reason why they would  feel goodwill or affection towards each  other which are absolutely central in  friendship now you know we could put it  in terms of complementarity and think  about romantic comedies and all of those  sorts of things though you you find  somebody who's very unlike you and they  introduce some spice to your life  because you're a straight-laced person  and they're the punker  and vice versa you know they get your  the the spontaneous artistic person and  they provide order to the chaos of your  life well sister all would would still  lump that in with this rather mercenary  cynical transactional view on things and  he suggests that there's actually a  better explanation so he raises this  this question and he says the more I  reflect on friendship the more it seems  to me consideration should be given to  this question whether the longing for  friendship  the desire for having friends is felt on  account of weakness and want so that by  the giving and receiving of favors one  may get from another and in turn repay  what he is unable to procure of himself  that's one alternative the way that he's  putting it or here's the other  alternative you know he says although  this mutual interchange is really  inseparable from friendship you've got  to have that whether there is not  another cause an older cause a more  beautiful cause right one that he in  this translation emanates more directly  from nature in in in the Latin here it's  Mogga Sonora it's a Profeta that that it  arises or it's brought to being from  nature out of nature and now when when  Cicero says nature don't think you know  the way that some people frame it today  and this Darwinian  nature red in tooth and claw or what  evolutionary biologists and  psychologists and whoever evolutionary  things wants to frame it because that  would actually be a very truncated  co