eming plasticity you can find  cases of animals brought up with each  other very different species sometimes  not even you know mammals and other  mammals but mammals and birds that  somehow create a kind of friendship  where they they clearly enjoy each  other's company they have something in  common they take care of each other  they show affection to each other you  might think for example of the you see a  lot of these videos on YouTube the one  with the owl and the cat who would play  with each other or you know other  examples you can come up with countless  ones human beings do this in terms of  familial relationships not every family  is a good family of course but he says  there's an affection that we can find  even more evident in human beings from  the affection existing between children  and parents which cannot be destroyed  except by some acceptable crime and  again from that kindred impulse of love  which arises when we met someone and it  will take a look at that in a moment  now notice that Cicero is not saying  that it's an automatic thing this this  affection between parents and children  and it's something that has to grow  nowadays we think of it in terms of  attachment right and there could be  reasons why it doesn't work a terrible  crime abuse of the children neglect of  the children in that case children may  not in fact feel affection towards their  parents or they might feel the kind of  tragic affection which will never be  satisfied and there are some parents who  are genuinely horrible parents probably  shouldn't have  kids but in in most cases I think we can  say that that we see across cultures  across time this impulse there and  Cicero says this comes from nature it's  even more so in the case of people he  says who's who share with us some habits  and nature and and the word for habits  there is Maurice motor boss actually  here quiesce Komori boss at night hora  con guru ammos right and so that this  this notion of agreement or a 